Proves per-thread cell LAYOUT, INITIALIZATION and ADDRESSING (SPEC §11.11), not
rooting. A `@threadlocal` cell can never hold a root: #1402's untraced-type rule
makes `@threadlocal let s: string` E0082, so a traced reference cannot exist in
one, and the collector never scans these cells by construction. The program is
also straight-line — codegen emits `bit_rt_safepoint` only at loop back-edges —
so THIS PROGRAM'S OWN code reaches zero safepoints, confirmed by inspection (no
loop). Its evidence is the linked binary's arithmetic on the thread pointer.

`BIT_GC=stress` does not report zero for the whole process, though. This
program fails to build on aarch64-macos ("Mach-O: per-thread module state
(@threadlocal) is not emitted on this target" — pre-existing, unrelated to
this finding), but it is `linux-only` and DOES build there: measured on real
x86_64-linux hardware (`mustafa-desktop-wsl`, `bit` 0.4.1, 28 cores, via
`.claude/boxlock.sh run --need x86_64-linux`), 5/5 runs: `collections=31`,
matching ../roothostlinux's and ../sigpipelinux's measured floor on the same
host — stress.bit's documented worker-count-tracking scheduler floor
(runtime/sched/workerrun.bit's `Worker.run`). stdout was byte-identical to
`.expected` (`8 9 10 1 1 42`) on this run. None of those 31 collections fire
at a back-edge this program emitted, so they still verify nothing about the
thread-pointer arithmetic this program proves.
